  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039932/assets/b2dbc407d70f4e6d3cf35dd24e1a6fb7d463207631a0498595ed40550ac86433.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-5.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-5.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -5(x-3)({x}^{2}+x(2\\text{i})-x(2\\text{i})-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ -5(x-3)({x}^{2}+4) & = \\\\ -5({x}^{3}+4x-3{x}^{2}-12) & = \\\\ -5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-20x+60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-20x+60.$",
